#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""teslalinux-uplink: keep the car (and the app) on an uplink that actually reaches the internet.

The device can have several uplinks at once: the LTE modem, a second Wi-Fi card that joins a known
network (home, a phone's hotspot, a garage's Wi-Fi), Ethernet, a phone on USB. DHCP (NetworkManager on
Pi OS, dhcpcd on Alpine) gives each its own default route; the kernel would use the lowest metric
whether or not that link reaches anything. Underground the LTE modem keeps its link and its route but
no longer passes traffic, so the car would lose the internet although a Wi-Fi network is right there.

Every few seconds this checks each uplink through that interface alone (a TCP connection to two
public anycast addresses with SO_BINDTODEVICE) and installs one default route of its own, metric 5,
in front of the DHCP ones, through the best uplink that works:
  prefer "lte"  (default) LTE, phone, Ethernet, then Wi-Fi: Wi-Fi only while LTE has no internet
  prefer "wifi" Wi-Fi, Ethernet, then LTE, phone: a known Wi-Fi saves mobile data when in range
An uplink is down after 2 failed checks in a row; a preferred one that comes back takes over again
after 3 good checks, so a garage entrance does not flap the route. When nothing works the route stays
where it is. The DHCP routes stay untouched underneath; if the chosen link disappears the kernel drops
our route with it and the next check picks another. The car's NAT (nftables masquerade) follows the
route by itself.

Where NetworkManager does not run (Alpine), this also keeps wpa_supplicant running on the second Wi-Fi
card with the networks saved from the settings page (host/teslalinux-network renders
/etc/teslalinux/wpa_supplicant-client.conf); dhcpcd takes the lease. Under NetworkManager its own
profiles do that.

LTE modem recovery: a Huawei E3372 that lost the signal (underground) often never reconnects when the
signal comes back, until it is re-plugged. When LTE has had no internet for 90 s although the stick
reports coverage, a worker thread (the route checks go on) climbs a ladder, one step at a time, at
least 2 minutes apart, each one given time to work:
  1. reconnect: HiLink mobile data off and on (its web API at the gateway / 192.168.8.1);
     ModemManager: disconnect, then NetworkManager's profile up (or mmcli --simple-connect with the APN)
  2. reset the modem: HiLink reboot (/api/device/control); ModemManager mmcli --reset
  3. USB re-enumeration (sysfs authorized 0/1; unbind/bind as fallback); also the first step when the
     HiLink API does not answer or asks for a login
  4. USB power cycle with uhubctl (a Pi 4 switches all four ports together): LTE_RECOVERY_POWER_CYCLE
     auto (default: only when the stick is the only USB device) | on | off
After the whole ladder it waits 15 minutes and starts again; working LTE resets it. No coverage: it
waits. With prefer "wifi" and a working Wi-Fi/Ethernet uplink it does nothing. LTE_RECOVERY=off in
/etc/teslalinux/env turns it off; LTE switched off in the settings (lte.env) too.

Settings: /etc/teslalinux/wifi-client.json (written by teslalinux-network: enabled, prefer, iface, the
networks), /etc/teslalinux/env (LTE_RECOVERY, LTE_RECOVERY_POWER_CYCLE), /etc/teslalinux/lte.env (APN).
State for the settings page's Status: /run/teslalinux/uplink.json (with a "modem" object).
"""
